course: arts
lesson: 39
title: Art as a job: who makes art, money, fame and hard work
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Behind every song, film, cartoon and painting are people doing a job: musicians, actors, animators, buskers, painters and designers, and most of them are not rich or famous.
explain: Fame and money are not the same thing: a song with a billion plays earns a lot, but each play pays only a tiny bit of a penny, shared between many people.
explain: "Overnight" stars usually trained for years where nobody could see, like a tree that grows roots for a long time before anyone notices it above the ground.
explain: Artists also need school skills, reading, maths and how to handle money and deals, because making art for a living is also running a tiny business.
explain: People say it their own way: "merch" means T-shirts and other things artists sell, "gurt" in the West Country means very, "abeg" in Nigeria means please, "hombre" in Spanish means man or mate, and "sef" in Nigerian Pidgin adds "even".
fact: Streaming apps pay a tiny amount for each play, often reported as a small part of a penny or cent, shared between the record company, songwriters, producers and the singer.
fact: Many musicians earn more from concerts and merch than from streams.
fact: It is often said that Van Gogh sold only one painting in his life; historians say he may have sold a few more, but he was not famous until after he died in 1890.
fact: Van Gogh's sister-in-law, Jo van Gogh-Bonger, worked hard to make his paintings known after his death.
fact: Most artists need reading, maths and business skills to manage money and deals, and many have other jobs, especially at first.
fact: K-pop singers usually train for years before their first show, and many trainees never become stars at all.
fact: Busking means performing in public places for tips; many cities have rules or permits for it, and many famous musicians busked when they started.
fact: Kalush Orchestra won Eurovision for Ukraine in 2022 with the song "Stefania", and it was reported that they auctioned the trophy to raise money for Ukraine; Eurovision gives fame, not a big cash prize.
fact: Animators work on films, TV, games, adverts and apps, often in big teams; it is a real career.
fact: Taylor Swift re-recorded her early albums as "Taylor's Version" because the first recordings were sold without her control, and she wanted to own her music.
fact: An artist's price pays for years of learning, materials and the idea, not only the hour spent drawing.
fact: Most actors are not famous; many do small roles, adverts and voice work, and face many auditions and rejections.
fact: Very high art prices come from fame, history, rarity and buyers competing at auctions, not only from how hard a picture looks to make.
exercise: Noah thinks a billion streams makes a singer a billionaire. What is true? | Each play pays a tiny bit, shared among many people | Every single stream pays the singer one whole dollar | Streams pay nothing, so singers earn only from shops | The app gives the singer a billion dollars as a prize
exercise: Where do many musicians earn more money than from streams? | From concerts and merch | From adverts on their phone apps | From the government every month | From the radio stations buying their songs
exercise: Olena's teacher says Van Gogh sold almost nothing while alive. What is the right conclusion? | Not selling then did not mean he was bad | He was a bad painter who got lucky after | His paintings were all lost until last year | He never painted anything until he was old
exercise: Who helped make Van Gogh's paintings known after he died? | His sister-in-law, Jo van Gogh-Bonger | His best friend Michael Jordan | The king of the Netherlands | A museum robber who later felt sorry and returned all of his paintings
exercise: Liam wants to quit school to be a full-time artist. What is true? | Artists need reading, maths and money skills | Artists never need to read or count anything | Art schools take anyone who has left school | School is only for people who want office jobs
exercise: Sakura's cousin thinks K-pop stars got famous overnight. What is true? | Most trained for years, and many trainees never debut | They were picked from the street on the first day of trying | Fame always comes overnight to singers who are lucky | K-pop stars do not have to practise singing or dancing
exercise: Ben says the busker is "just begging with a guitar". Which is fairer? | Busking is work: performing for tips | Busking is not allowed in any city | Buskers are paid a wage by the town | Busking is only for people with no talent
exercise: What is busking? | Performing in public places for tips | Selling paintings in a big auction house | Recording songs in a studio for an app | Acting in a film as a background extra
exercise: Yuliia asks if Kalush Orchestra got rich from winning Eurovision. What is true? | Eurovision brings fame, not a big cash prize | The winners get a million euros in cash | The winners get a free house in every country | Winning means they never have to work again
exercise: What was reported about Kalush Orchestra's trophy? | It was auctioned to raise money for Ukraine | It was melted down to make new microphones | It was given back because they lost a vote | It was stolen from the stage after the show
exercise: Carlos's uncle says animators "just draw cartoons, not a proper job". What is true? | Animation is a real career in films, games and adverts | Animators are always unpaid and do it as a hobby | Only children can be animators | Animators only work on one cartoon in their whole lives
exercise: Why did Taylor Swift re-record her old albums? | She wanted to own her recordings | She forgot the words and wanted to fix them | Her old albums were all lost in a fire | Her fans asked her to sing them faster
exercise: Ali thinks 500 rupees is too much for one hour of drawing. What does the price pay for? | Years of learning, materials and the idea | Only the paper and pencil used | Only the one hour, nothing else | Nothing, because art should always be free for anybody who wants it
exercise: Mackenzie asks if all actors live in Hollywood mansions. What is true? | Most do small roles and other jobs, and few get rich | Every actor is a millionaire by the age of thirty | Actors are given a mansion when they finish school | Only actors with mansions are allowed to audition
exercise: Kayode says he could draw a painting that sold for millions. Why can prices be so high? | Fame, history, rarity and buyers bidding against each other | The paint used was made of real crushed diamonds | Prices are set by how long the painting took | Big prices are paid only for the biggest paintings in the whole auction
exercise: What is merch? | T-shirts and other things artists sell | A kind of song played at concerts | The money a song earns from each play | A ticket for a music festival
test: Your mate goes, "Billion streams, so he's a billionaire, no cap." What is the best answer? | Each stream pays a tiny amount, split many ways | He is right, one stream pays one dollar | Streams pay more than concerts every time | Only singers who reach a billion streams ever get paid anything at all
test: What would happen if Liam left school at ten to be an artist? | He would miss skills he needs to run his art as a job | He would become rich faster than anyone else | Art schools would give him a free place at once | Nothing at all, because artists never use anything they learned at school
test: A Gen Z kid says Van Gogh "fell off" while alive ("fell off" means failed). Which is fairer? | He was not famous then, and fame came after his death | He was the richest painter in Europe in his lifetime | He stopped painting because nobody liked his work | He was only famous because his paintings were stolen
test: In a cartoon, a girl sings once in her bedroom and is a world star the next morning. What is more realistic? | Years of hidden practice usually come before fame | One song in a bedroom always makes you famous | Stars are chosen at random from a list | Fame comes first for most singers, and they learn how to sing afterwards
test: A Bristol lad calls a busker "gurt silly" ("gurt" means very). What is the fairer view? | Buskers work, practising and entertaining strangers | Buskers are breaking the law in every town | Buskers are paid by the shops they stand near | Busking needs no practice or skill at all, you just stand there and play
test: Yuliia screams "we won!" when Kalush Orchestra wins. Which is true? | Ukraine won Eurovision in 2022 with "Stefania" | Ukraine has never won Eurovision | The band won a big cash prize for each member | The song was sung in Spanish
test: A Madrid uncle says, "Animators just doodle, hombre" ("hombre" means mate). How could Carlos show the job is real? | Point at the long credits of an animated film | Show him a doodle on a school book | Say all jobs are just doodling | Agree with his uncle and give up on animation to find a proper job instead
test: A kid in Pennsylvania says Taylor Swift re-recording is "sus, she's just milking it" ("sus" means suspicious). What did she explain? | It was about owning her own recordings | She lost her voice and wanted a new one | She had to by law every five years | Her old songs were banned on the radio
test: Ali's friend's drawing took one hour. Why is 500 rupees fair? | The price covers years of learning and materials too | One hour of anything always costs 500 rupees | Drawings are priced by the size of the paper | The friend added the cost of his lunch and his bus fare to the price
test: Kayode says, "Abeg, I fit draw that one sef" (abeg means please, sef means even). Which is true? | The price comes from fame, history and demand, not only skill | The painting must be secretly made of gold | Anyone who can copy it gets the same price | The auction house pays the artist's family twice
test: Mackenzie says "all actors are rich, eh" ("eh" means right?). Which is true? | Most actors face many auditions and do small roles | All actors are rich from their very first film | Actors never have to audition for parts | Actors live for free inside the film studios where they make their films
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a song streamed a billion times does not yield a billion dollars to its singer. | Each play yields a small fraction of a cent, divided among several parties | Streaming services are prohibited from paying singers | Each play yields exactly one dollar, retained by the app | Singers receive payment solely for live performances and never for any recordings
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises Van Gogh's reputation during his lifetime? | He was little known and sold very few paintings | He was the most celebrated painter in Europe | He sold several hundred paintings each year | He was famous chiefly as a musician
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the prize for winning the Eurovision Song Contest. | It confers fame rather than a substantial cash prize | It confers one million euros upon each performer | It confers a residence in every participating country | It confers a lifelong exemption from employment
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which Taylor Swift re-recorded her early albums. | To secure ownership of her own recordings | To correct words that she had forgotten | To comply with a law requiring re-recording | To replace recordings destroyed by fire
